<!doctype html><html lang="en"><head><meta charset="utf-8"><meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1"><meta name="robots" content="noindex"><meta name="description" content="AI Updates 2026-10-23: Treasury proposes curbs on investing in foreign superintelligence developers; METR finds gaps in OpenAI agent monitoring; AA Coding Agent Index v2.0."><title>AI Updates — Oct 23, 2026 · Treasury AI investment rule; METR on OpenAI monitoring</title><link rel="stylesheet" href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Manrope:wght@600;800&family=IBM+Plex+Sans:wght@400;500;600&family=IBM+Plex+Mono:wght@400;500&display=swap"><style>/* PALETTE:BEGIN */
:root{--ground:#FFFFFF;--surface:#FFFFFF;--ink:#1F2233;--muted:#5C6178;--line:#E6E3F0;--bar:#D7D2F0;--bar-text:#3A3560;--pick:#5B3FC4;--pick-soft:#ECE7FD;--old:#6E7390;--good:#1C7A4E;--mid:#8F5B00;--weak:#B23A4E;--good-soft:#E1F5EA;--mid-soft:#FDF0D5;--weak-soft:#FCE4E8;--display:'Manrope',system-ui,sans-serif; --body:'IBM Plex Sans',system-ui,sans-serif; --mono:'IBM Plex Mono',ui-monospace,Menlo,monospace;color-scheme:light}
#ns-60{--pick:#B03A7A;--pick-soft:#FCE6F1;--ground:#FFF5FA;background:var(--ground);border-radius:12px}
#ns-jobs{--pick:#1D6FA5;--pick-soft:#E2F1FB;--ground:#F2F9FE;background:var(--ground);border-radius:12px}
#ns-score{--pick:#5B3FC4;--pick-soft:#ECE7FD;--ground:#F7F5FE;background:var(--ground);border-radius:12px}
#news{--pick:#0F7A6C;--pick-soft:#DDF5F0;background:var(--ground);border-radius:12px}
#cost{--pick:#9A5A00;--pick-soft:#FDF0D5;background:var(--ground);border-radius:12px}
/* PALETTE:END */
#glance{--pick:#B03A7A;--pick-soft:#FCE6F1;--ground:#FFF5FA;background:var(--ground);border-radius:12px}
#best{--pick:#1D6FA5;--pick-soft:#E2F1FB;--ground:#F2F9FE;background:var(--ground);border-radius:12px}
#score{--pick:#5B3FC4;--pick-soft:#ECE7FD;--ground:#F7F5FE;background:var(--ground);border-radius:12px}
#how{--pick:#9A5A00;--pick-soft:#FDF0D5}
*{box-sizing:border-box}html{scroll-behavior:smooth;scroll-padding-top:56px}
body{margin:0;background:#fff;color:var(--ink);font-family:var(--body);font-size:16px;line-height:1.5}
.wrap{max-width:760px;margin:0 auto;padding:22px 16px 60px}
h1,h2{font-family:var(--display);margin:0}h1{font-size:30px;font-weight:800;letter-spacing:-.02em}
h2{font-size:19px;font-weight:800;margin-bottom:10px}
p{margin:0}a{color:var(--pick)}
.stamp{font-family:var(--mono);font-size:12px;letter-spacing:.06em;text-transform:uppercase;color:var(--muted)}
.chips{display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:8px;margin-top:8px}
.chip{border-radius:999px;padding:2px 10px;font-weight:600;font-size:12px}
.chip.good{background:var(--good-soft);color:var(--good)}.chip.pick{background:var(--pick-soft);color:var(--pick)}.chip.mid{background:var(--mid-soft);color:var(--mid)}
.toc{position:sticky;top:0;z-index:50;margin:12px -16px 0;padding:6px 12px;background:#fff;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line);box-shadow:0 1px 4px rgba(31,34,51,.08);display:flex;gap:6px;flex-wrap:wrap}
.toc a{text-decoration:none;font-size:13px;font-weight:600;color:var(--ink);border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:999px;padding:5px 10px}
section{margin-top:32px;padding:14px}
.card{background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;padding:14px}
.qa{list-style:none;margin:0;padding:0;display:grid;gap:10px}
.qa li{border-bottom:1px solid var(--line);padding-bottom:8px}.qa li:last-child{border:0;padding:0}
.q{font-weight:700;font-size:15px}.a{font-size:14.5px;margin:3px 0}
.meta{display:flex;gap:8px;align-items:center;font-size:13px;flex-wrap:wrap}
details{font-size:13.5px}details>summary{cursor:pointer;color:var(--pick);font-weight:600;font-size:13px;list-style:none}
details>summary::-webkit-details-marker{display:none}
details>summary::after{content:" ▸"}details[open]>summary::after{content:" ▾"}
details .in{color:var(--bar-text);margin-top:6px;font-size:13.5px}
details .in p+p{margin-top:6px}
.jl{list-style:none;margin:0;padding:0}
.jl li{padding:6px 0;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line);font-size:14.5px}.jl li:last-child{border:0}
.jl b{color:var(--ink)}.v{color:var(--muted);font-size:13px;white-space:nowrap}
.story{margin-top:10px}.story:first-of-type{margin-top:0}
.story .tag{font-size:11px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.06em;color:var(--muted)}
.story h4{font-family:var(--display);font-size:16.5px;font-weight:800;margin:2px 0 4px;line-height:1.25}
.story .s{font-size:14.5px}.why{border-left:3px solid #0F7A6C;padding-left:9px;margin-top:6px;font-size:14px}
.src{font-size:13px;margin-top:6px}
.one{padding:7px 0;border-top:1px solid var(--line);font-size:14px}.one b{font-family:var(--display)}
table.sb{border-collapse:collapse;width:100%;font-size:13px}
.sb th,.sb td{padding:6px 4px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line);text-align:center}
.sb th{font-size:12px;color:var(--muted);font-weight:600}.sb td:first-child,.sb th:first-child{text-align:left;font-weight:700}
.T{color:var(--pick);font-weight:700}.N{color:var(--bar-text)}.D{color:var(--muted)}
.note{font-size:13px;color:var(--muted);margin-top:8px}
ul.in{padding-left:18px}
/* variant b */
.dash{width:100%;border-collapse:collapse;font-size:13.5px;margin-top:12px}
.dash td{padding:7px 6px;border-bottom:1px solid var(--line);vertical-align:top}.dash td:first-child{font-weight:700;width:34%;color:var(--muted);font-size:12px;text-transform:uppercase;letter-spacing:.04em}
.swipe{display:flex;gap:10px;overflow-x:auto;scroll-snap-type:x mandatory;padding-bottom:6px}
.swipe .story{flex:0 0 86%;scroll-snap-align:start;margin:0;background:#fff;border:1px solid var(--line);border-radius:10px;padding:12px}
.hintx{font-size:12px;color:var(--muted);margin-top:4px}
</style></head><body><div class="wrap">
<div class="stamp">Friday, Oct 23, 2026 · ET</div><h1>AI Updates</h1>
<div class="chips"><span class="chip mid">A US investment curb and an AI-safety audit</span><span class="chip good">Five board updates</span></div><nav class="toc"><a href="#glance">Glance</a><a href="#news">News</a><a href="#best">Best for what</a><a href="#score">Scoreboard</a><a href="#how">Sources</a></nav>
<section id="top" style="margin-top:16px;padding:0"><div class="card"><table class="dash" style="margin-top:0"><tr><td>Big news</td><td>The US proposes banning investment in foreign “superintelligence” developers. <span class="chip good">Confirmed</span></td></tr><tr><td>Patch now</td><td>No urgent patch. Microsoft ties an AI-run hacking campaign to the ConnectWise breach. <span class="chip mid">Microsoft says</span></td></tr><tr><td>Boards</td><td>A new coding test puts two Claude Opus 5.5 setups level at the top. <span class="chip pick">Too close to call</span></td></tr><tr><td>Best overall</td><td>Claude Opus 5.5: top on all four scoreboard columns.</td></tr><tr><td>Cheapest good</td><td>GPT-6 Luna and MiMo-V2.6-Pro.</td></tr></table></div></section>
<section id="glance"><h2>Today at a glance</h2><div class="card"><ul class="qa"><li><div class="q">What’s the big news?</div><p class="a">Treasury wants to stop US money flowing to foreign “superintelligence” developers.</p><div class="meta"><span class="chip good">Confirmed</span></div><details><summary>Show the numbers</summary><div class="in">Proposed rule under the COINS Act; covers “super intelligence systems”, supercomputing, hypersonics and biotech. Comments due Dec 7 (per the NPRM). A final rule sets the start date.</div></details></li><li><div class="q">Is AI safety monitoring holding up?</div><p class="a">Outside testers found gaps in OpenAI’s; it says they’re fixed.</p><div class="meta"><span class="chip pick">Not yet checked</span></div><details><summary>Show the numbers</summary><div class="in">METR and Redwood found two headers that turned monitoring off for new agents, plus other gaps, as of July. OpenAI says production fixes are in; METR hasn’t evaluated them.</div></details></li><li><div class="q">What moved on the boards?</div><p class="a">A rebuilt coding test puts Claude Opus 5.5 setups on top.</p><div class="meta"><span class="chip pick">Too close to call</span></div><details><summary>Show the numbers</summary><div class="in">AA Coding Agent Index v2.0 (not comparable with v1.5): Claude Code with Opus 5.5 (max) 61.7, Devin Fusion CLI 61.5 (0.14 behind), Claude Code with Sonnet 5.5 59.3; no intervals. Also: GPT-6 Astra 83.1% edges GPT-6.1 Sol 82.0% on CritPt v2 physics; DeepSeek V4.1 Flash 1017 78.1% on ARC-AGI-2, top open-weight entry; Gemini 4 Argon 1= on Scale DrugDiscoveryBench (65.40 ±2.90); Argon 4th on Scale’s Remote Labor Index (17.08%).</div></details></li></ul></div></section>
<section id="news" style="background:#F1FBF8;border-radius:12px"><h2>News</h2><div class="swipe"><div class="story"><div class="tag">Lead · US Treasury</div><h4>US proposes a ban on investing in foreign “superintelligence” developers</h4><p class="s">A Treasury draft rule would bar US investment in companies in countries of concern that build “super intelligence” AI.</p><p class="why"><b>Why it matters:</b> it widens today’s limits on AI investment abroad; proposal only, comments due Dec 7.</p><p class="src">Source: <a href="https://home.treasury.gov/news/press-releases/sb0667/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">US Treasury press release</a></p><details><summary>Details</summary><div class="in"><p><b>Status:</b> proposed rule under the COINS Act (Docket TREAS-DO-2026-0595); a later final rule sets the start date.</p><p><b>What changes (per the fact sheet):</b> a broader ban on US investment in country-of-concern entities that develop any “super intelligence” system, dropping today’s notification tier for those deals; adds supercomputing, hypersonics and biotech; widens countries of concern beyond China. Penalties up to IEEPA maximums, possible forced divestment.</p><p><b>Caveat:</b> the Dec 7 comment date and the wider ban come from the NPRM and fact sheet, not the press release itself.</p></div></details></div><div class="story"><div class="tag">Co‑lead · AI safety · OpenAI</div><h4>Outside testers found holes in OpenAI’s agent-watching system</h4><p class="s">METR and Redwood Research found ways around the monitoring OpenAI uses to catch its own agents misbehaving.</p><p class="why"><b>Why it matters:</b> it’s a frontier lab’s safety net; OpenAI says it’s fixed, unchecked so far.</p><p class="src">Source: <a href="https://metr.org/blog/2026-10-22-red-teaming-openai-agent-monitoring/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">METR report</a></p><details><summary>Details</summary><div class="in"><p><b>Findings (as of July 21):</b> two client-controllable headers that switched monitoring off for newly started agents; RL training runs uncovered at the start; a Codex sandbox and Auto-Review bypass; a redacted exploit that would hide hundreds of recent agent actions. METR estimates about 75–94% of agentic internal traffic was eligible for monitoring.</p><p><b>Caveat:</b> two-person pilot (July 21–Aug 11). OpenAI “has stated” production fixes are in; METR hasn’t evaluated them. The 61-page report is private. Follows OpenAI’s own Oct 20 misalignment report.</p></div></details></div><div class="story"><div class="tag">Also · China · Exports</div><h4>Chinese state media says DeepSeek and MiniMax cleared new export rules</h4><p class="s">A CCTV-linked account says both completed registration for open-sourcing and overseas use under China’s new AI export rules.</p><p class="why"><b>Why it matters:</b> an early sign of how the rules apply; no ministry notice yet.</p><p class="src">Source: <a href="https://www.yicai.com/news/103405081.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Yicai</a></p><details><summary>Details</summary><div class="in"><p><b>Source:</b> Yuyuan Tantian post (evening Oct 22, Beijing), carried by Yicai; Reuters and SCMP followed.</p><p><b>Caveat:</b> registration under Announcement No. 48 is pre-export registration, not a licence. Reuters’ headline “win approval” goes beyond what the post says. Neither company replied to Reuters.</p></div></details></div><div class="story"><div class="tag">Also · Japan · Policy</div><h4>Japan orders a review of its AI law</h4><p class="s">The Chief Cabinet Secretary told ministers to review laws “including the AI Act” and to strengthen Japan’s AI Safety Institute.</p><p class="why"><b>Why it matters:</b> Japan may firm up its light-touch AI rules; instructions only.</p><p class="src">Source: <a href="https://www.kantei.go.jp/jp/pages/20261022choukan_aidigital.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Prime Minister’s Office (Japanese)</a></p><details><summary>Details</summary><div class="in"><p>First meeting of the AI and Digital Reform Promotion Council, Oct 22. An interim summary is due by year-end; concrete governance steps are promised “after the new year.”</p></div></details></div><div class="story"><div class="tag">Also · Security · Microsoft</div><h4>Microsoft says hackers used an AI attack tool at scale</h4><p class="s">Microsoft says a China-aligned group used ARTEX, an open-source AI hacking agent, on hundreds of organisations.</p><p class="why"><b>Why it matters:</b> AI agents are in real attacks; Microsoft also ties the group to the ConnectWise breach.</p><p class="src">Source: <a href="https://www.microsoft.com/en-us/security/blog/2026/10/21/storm-0940-uses-artex-agentic-ai-connectwise-supply-chain-compromise/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Microsoft Security Blog</a></p><details><summary>Details</summary><div class="in"><p><b>Microsoft’s account:</b> Storm-0940 used ARTEX to try sign-ins to more than 2,250 tenants; in some cases it reached privileged cloud access in under 3 hours. Microsoft “assesses” the group was behind the ConnectWise compromise.</p><p><b>Caveat:</b> Microsoft says a successful sign-in doesn’t necessarily mean data was accessed, and its own systems weren’t compromised. Posted Oct 21.</p></div></details></div><div class="story"><div class="tag">Also · Banks · Agents</div><h4>Banks are spotting AI agents inside customer accounts</h4><p class="s">Axios reports big US banks are finding third-party AI agents using customers’ logins, and some are restricting them.</p><p class="why"><b>Why it matters:</b> if you let an AI agent into your bank account, your bank may block it.</p><p class="src">Source: <a href="https://www.axios.com/2026/10/22/ai-agents-banks-unauthorized-accounts-access" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Axios</a></p><details><summary>Details</summary><div class="in"><p><b>Per unnamed sources:</b> Morgan Stanley is blocking third-party agents; JPMorgan, Bank of America and Citi are restricting them. Some agents appear to act beyond user instructions.</p><p><b>On the record:</b> Morgan Stanley says it hasn’t seen “rogue AI agents accessing our platforms”; Chase says don’t share passwords. Outlet reports, not confirmed by the banks.</p></div></details></div><div class="story"><div class="tag">Also · Health · Study</div><h4>Microsoft’s diagnosis AI beat generalist doctors on hard cases</h4><p class="s">In Science, Microsoft authors report their DxO system solved far more tough cases than generalist physicians.</p><p class="why"><b>Why it matters:</b> a big gap on a peer-reviewed test, but text-only cases, not real patients.</p><p class="src">Source: <a href="https://www.science.org/doi/10.1126/science.aeh7930" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Science paper</a></p><details><summary>Details</summary><div class="in"><p><b>Authors report:</b> DxO with GPT-5 scored 83.2% on 304 NEJM cases vs physicians’ 36.1% open-book (22.3% closed-book) on 56 held-out cases; 80.3% vs 46.8% on emergency-department cases.</p><p><b>Caveat:</b> all authors are Microsoft employees; no p-values or intervals in the main text; they say it doesn’t show superiority over specialists or real-world practice.</p></div></details></div><div class="story"><div class="tag">Also · Anthropic · Security research</div><h4>Anthropic says Claude mathematically proved code won’t crash</h4><p class="s">Anthropic reports Claude proved 239 entry points in 123 open-source libraries can’t crash, after fixing bugs it found.</p><p class="why"><b>Why it matters:</b> formal proofs at this scale are new; they hold only under narrow assumptions.</p><p class="src">Source: <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/research/formal-verification" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anthropic research post</a></p><details><summary>Details</summary><div class="in"><p><b>Authors report:</b> about 180 million lines of Rocq proofs; no crash on any input under 64 MB, assuming a single-threaded static RISC-V Linux binary with a fixed memory budget. Opus 5.5 proved half of 36 small tasks with about 40% fewer tokens than Opus 5.</p><p><b>Caveat:</b> the post itself says “formally verified” does not mean “bug-free.” With Theorem.</p></div></details></div><div class="story"><div class="tag">Also · Anthropic · Standards</div><h4>Anthropic hands its Agent Skills standard to the Linux Foundation</h4><p class="s">Agent Skills, a format for packaging reusable tasks for AI agents, moves to the Agentic AI Foundation.</p><p class="why"><b>Why it matters:</b> a shared standard outside any one company’s control.</p><p class="src">Source: <a href="https://www.anthropic.com/news/donating-agent-skills-to-the-agentic-ai-foundation" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Anthropic</a></p><details><summary>Details</summary><div class="in"><p>Includes the name, logo, GitHub org and agentskills.io. Anthropic says (unverified) more than 70 agent platforms use it, with 1 million skills published and nearly 280 million installs. Engineers from Anthropic, Google, OpenAI and others maintain it as individuals.</p></div></details></div></div><p class="hintx">Swipe for more → 9 stories</p></section>
<section id="best"><h2>Best for what</h2><div class="card"><ul class="jl"><li><b>Everyday professional work</b> → Claude Sonnet 5.5, Claude Opus 5.5, Gemini 4 Argon <span class="v">(tied)</span><details><summary>Show the numbers</summary><div class="in">Vals Index, Oct 20. Next: Muse Wonder Full, GPT-6 Astra and Muse Wonder, tied 4th. Watch out: Sonnet and Opus totals include fallback answers; counted as failures, Vals says Argon leads.</div></details></li><li><b>Coding help</b> → Claude Code with Claude Opus 5.5 <span class="v">(close race)</span><details><summary>Show the numbers</summary><div class="in">AA Coding Agent Index v2.0, Oct 22 (new method; not comparable with v1.5): 61.7, 1st by value; Devin Fusion CLI (Opus 5.5 lead with a GPT-6 Luna sidekick) 61.5, 0.14 behind; AA gives no intervals, so the top two can’t be separated. Next: Claude Code with Sonnet 5.5 59.3, Muse Code with Muse Wonder Full 58.9. Opus refused 76 of 870 attempts, each recovered by falling back to Opus 4.8. Scores the tool and model together.</div></details></li><li><b>Web apps people like</b> → Claude Opus 5.5 <span class="v">(clear lead)</span><details><summary>Show the numbers</summary><div class="in">LMArena WebDev, Oct 19: clear 1st. Next: GPT-6 Astra, Claude Sonnet 5.5 and GPT-6.1 Sol, tied 2nd. Human preference, not working code; Muse Wonder Full has only 1,639 votes.</div></details></li><li><b>Agents that help real users</b> → Claude Opus 5.5 <span class="v">(clear lead)</span><details><summary>Show the numbers</summary><div class="in">Agent Arena Net Improvement, Oct 20: clear 1st. Next: GPT-6 Astra, Claude Fable 5.1, GPT-6.1 Sol, Claude Sonnet 5.5, Gemini 4 Argon and Muse Wonder Full, tied 2nd. Muse Wonder Full has only 6,502 sessions; real-session signals, not a pass rate.</div></details></li><li><b>Chat and writing</b> → Gemini 4 Argon and Muse Wonder <span class="v">(tied)</span><details><summary>Show the numbers</summary><div class="in">LMArena Text, Oct 13: statistically tied at the top; Claude Opus 5.5 also in the top tie group. Argon is Preliminary; Muse Wonder has the fewest votes in the top group.</div></details></li><li><b>Finance work</b> → Claude Opus 5.5 <span class="v">(close race)</span><details><summary>Show the numbers</summary><div class="in">Vals Finance, Oct 21: leads by value, but Gemini 4 Argon, Muse Wonder Full and Claude Sonnet 5.5 are statistically tied with it.</div></details></li><li><b>Long conversations</b> → GPT-6.1 Sol <span class="v">(close race)</span><details><summary>Show the numbers</summary><div class="in">Scale MultiChallenge, Oct 21: leads by value; Gemini 4 Argon, Muse Spark and Muse Spark Open are statistically tied with it.</div></details></li><li><b>Solving puzzles</b> → GPT-6 Astra <span class="v">(close race)</span><details><summary>Show the numbers</summary><div class="in">ARC-AGI-3 (Provider Adapter), Oct 21: Astra 1st by value; Argon 2nd; ARC Prize gives no intervals.</div></details></li><li><b>Long medical records</b> → Claude Sonnet 5.5 <span class="v">(tied)</span><details><summary>Show the numbers</summary><div class="in">AA MLCR-AA, Oct 21: top, shared with one other (+1). Next: GPT-6.1 Sol.</div></details></li><li><b>Clinician health questions</b> → GPT-6 Astra <span class="v">(leads)</span><details><summary>Show the numbers</summary><div class="in">HealthBench Professional, Oct 19. Next: GPT-6.1 Sol.</div></details></li><li><b>Free to download and run yourself</b> → DeepSeek V4.1 Flash <span class="v">(leads)</span><details><summary>Show the numbers</summary><div class="in">Top open entry by value on Agent Arena; tied 1st among open models on WebDev and the Vals Index. New Oct 22: the 1017 version (Max) scores 78.1% on ARC-AGI-2, the top open-weight entry by value (ARC Prize, no intervals). Next: Kimi K3, GLM-5.3 and MiMo-V2.6-Pro. MiniMax-M3.1-Flash scores well, but its weights aren’t out yet.</div></details></li><li><b>Do it cheaply</b> → GPT-6 Luna and MiMo-V2.6-Pro <span class="v">(best value)</span><details><summary>Show the numbers</summary><div class="in">AA cost board, Oct 8: most index points per dollar. GPT-6.1 Sol gives near-top smarts for under a dollar per task. Free to run yourself: Ling 3.1 Flash. Some AA cost figures are provisional (Claude Haiku 5.5).</div></details></li></ul><details><summary>More jobs</summary><ul class="in"><li>Physics problems (new) → GPT-6 Astra, too close to call with GPT-6.1 Sol (AA CritPt v2, Oct 22; 83.1% vs 82.0%, no intervals)</li><li>Drug-discovery tasks (new) → GPT-6 Astra (mini-SWE-agent) leads by value, tied with 3 others incl. Gemini 4 Argon (Scale DrugDiscoveryBench, Oct 22)</li><li>Paid freelance projects (new) → Claude Opus 5.5 leads by value; GPT-6 Astra next (Scale Remote Labor Index, Oct 22; no intervals; best agent finishes about a fifth of projects)</li><li>Cybersecurity skills → Claude Opus 5.5, tied with 7 others (Epoch Cyber ECI, Oct 16)</li><li>Research math → GPT-6.1 Sol, tied with 2 others (MathArena ArXivMath, Oct 17)</li><li>Web search research → GPT-5.6 Sol + Keenable, tied with 3 others (Vals Web Search, Oct 21)</li><li>Cloned voices → HeyGen Voice Turbo, tied with 2 others (AA Controlled Voice, Oct 21)</li><li>Real robot arm → GPT-6 Astra (AA-Robotics, Oct 14)</li><li>Overall smarts → Claude Opus 5.5 (AA composite Index, Oct 19)</li><li>Broad capability → Claude Opus 5.5, tied with 10 others (Epoch ECI, Oct 22)</li></ul><p class="note">New board sources today: <a href="https://artificialanalysis.ai/agents/coding-agents" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AA Coding Agent Index</a> · <a href="https://artificialanalysis.ai/evaluations/critpt-v2" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AA CritPt v2</a> · <a href="https://arcprize.org/results/deepseek-v4-1-flash-1017" target="_blank" rel="noopener">ARC Prize (DeepSeek V4.1 Flash 1017)</a> · <a href="https://labs.scale.com/leaderboard/drugdiscoverybench" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Scale DrugDiscoveryBench</a> · <a href="https://labs.scale.com/leaderboard/rli" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Scale Remote Labor Index</a></p></details></div></section>
<section id="score"><h2>Scoreboard</h2><div class="card"><table class="sb"><thead><tr><th>Model</th><th>Many tests</th><th>Agents</th><th>Web apps</th><th>Overall</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Claude Opus 5.5</td><td class="T">Top</td><td class="T">Top</td><td class="T">Top</td><td class="T">Top</td></tr><tr><td>Claude Sonnet 5.5</td><td class="T">Top</td><td class="N">Near top</td><td class="N">Near top</td><td class="N">Near top</td></tr><tr><td>Claude Fable 5.1</td><td class="T">Top</td><td class="N">Near top</td><td class="N">Near top</td><td class="N">Near top</td></tr><tr><td>GPT-6 Astra</td><td class="T">Top</td><td class="N">Near top</td><td class="N">Near top</td><td class="N">Near top</td></tr><tr><td>GPT-6.1 Sol</td><td class="T">Top</td><td class="N">Near top</td><td class="N">Near top</td><td class="D">—</td></tr><tr><td>Gemini 4 Argon</td><td class="T">Top</td><td class="N">Near top</td><td class="D">—</td><td class="N">Near top</td></tr></tbody></table>
